# Lesson 4, batch 2: a double finesse against the ace and queen. KJT4 opposite 832 at no-trump; duck the first heart, then lead toward dummy twice. The ten loses to East's ace, the jack wins.
title: Toward the king-jack-ten
id: L04-25
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.5
student: S
board: 103
dealer: S
vulnerable: both

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  864    A52    Q73    KJT4
  E  QJT9   64     T852   A65
  S  AK5    K73    AK96   832
  W  732    QJT98  J4     Q97

auction:
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 8
  spades: 2 = The ace and king.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king.
  diamonds: 3 = The ace, king and queen. A fourth only if they split 3-3.
  clubs: 1 = One, once the ace and queen are gone. Two if West has the queen and you lead toward dummy twice.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: To set up the clubs, you'll lose the lead at least once. What will the defense lead when they get in?
play:
  1   W HQ  N H2  E H6  S H3*
      note: Eight obvious winners. Clubs can give a ninth: lead toward dummy's king-jack-ten twice, and you make two club tricks whenever West has the queen. But you'll lose the lead to the ♣A first, and West's hearts are the danger.
      H3 key: Duck. You'll have to lose the lead anyway, so lose a heart now. Then East may have no heart left to lead back.
      H3 not HK: Did you think about ducking? Win now, and when East gets in with the ♣A, East leads a heart and knocks out your last stopper. Lose a heart now, while it costs nothing.
  2   W HJ  N H5  E H4  S HK
      note: Win in your hand: you want to lead clubs from there, and dummy's ♥A is still a stopper.
  3   S C2  W C7  N CT*  E CA
      note: Lead a club toward dummy.
      CT key: West played low, so finesse the ten. If West has the queen, the ten forces out the ace, and the queen is still trapped.
      CT not CK: Did you think about finessing? The king wins only if West has the ace. Finesse the ten, and then the jack: you make two club tricks whenever West has the queen.
  4   E SQ  S SA  W S2  N S4
      note: The ten lost to the ace, but East has no heart to lead. Back in your hand, lead another club.
  5   S C3  W C9  N CJ*  E C5
      CJ key: West played low again, so finesse the jack. If West has the queen, it wins.
      CJ not CK: Did you notice West may still have the queen? Finesse again with the jack.
  6   N CK  E C6  S C8  W CQ
      note: The jack won. Cash the king, and West's queen falls.
  7   N C4  E ST  S S5  W S3
      note: Dummy's last club is a winner too.
  8   N HA  E D2  S H7  W H9
  9   N D3  E D5  S DA  W D4
      note: Now the diamonds.
  10  S DK  W DJ  N D7  E D8
  11  S D6  W S7  N DQ  E DT
      note: That's ten tricks.
  12  N S6  E SJ  S SK  W H8
  13  S D9  W HT  N S8  E S9
      note: 11 tricks: 3NT made with two overtricks.

play recap:
  The plan: eight obvious winners, and two club tricks if West held the
  queen and you led toward dummy's king-jack-ten twice.

  Before you touched the clubs, you ducked a heart. When East won the ♣A,
  East had no heart left to lead, so West's hearts never got going. Then the
  second finesse won, because West had the queen. This double finesse works
  whenever West has the queen. If East held both the ace and the queen, you
  would make only one club trick.
